          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does adherence mean in the context of diabetes treatment, and why does it matter?",
          "answer": "In the context of diabetes treatment, adherence refers to how well a person follows their recommended treatment plan. This includes things like taking prescribed medications, monitoring blood glucose levels, and making lifestyle adjustments such as diet and physical activity.\n\nAdherence is crucial in diabetes because diabetes is largely a self-managed condition. Most decisions that affect the outcome are made by the individual between appointments. Poor adherence to glucose-lowering medication is common. Factors that affect adherence include cost, treatment complexity, side effects, low health literacy, depression, and simply forgetting. Each of these factors may require a different approach to improve adherence.\n\nStructured education and support are considered a core part of diabetes care to help improve adherence, glucose control, and quality of life. This information comes from the document \"Sticking with a diabetes regimen: monitoring, medication and review\" (National diabetes association and self-management education guidance).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
